{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—too rhythmic, too clean—and when he sat up, the grass beneath him shimmered faintly, like a texture mapped onto a low-poly model. His fingers brushed the ground and a translucent HUD flickered into existence at the edge of his vision: HP 100/100 | Stamina 87% | Quest: “Find the Whispering Oak.” He blinked hard, rubbed his eyes, but the interface remained, stubborn and silent. His phone, when he pulled it from his pocket, displayed no signal—just a pixelated battery icon and a countdown timer labeled “Day 7 of 30.” The trees around him swayed in identical arcs, their branches never deviating, never rustling with wind that didn’t exist. A squirrel darted past, paused mid-leap, then rewound its motion backward before repeating the sequence again.\n\n***\n\nHe found the Whispering Oak at noon, its bark etched with glowing runes that pulsed like a heartbeat. As he reached out, a voice crackled from nowhere—calm, synthetic, familiar. “Welcome back, Player 7342. You’ve been offline for 147 days.” He froze. That was his old gaming handle. His real name hadn’t been used since college. The tree’s leaves rustled, not with wind, but with text scrolling in ancient code. “You chose to log out after your wife’s accident,” the voice continued. “The simulation was designed to heal you. But you forgot you were playing.” He looked down at his hands—now slightly translucent at the edges—and realized he hadn’t cried in months. Not because he was numb. But because, in this world, grief was a glitch he’d programmed himself to ignore.", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.6198107600212097}, {"token": " woke", "logprob": -0.060740288347005844}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.020681682974100113}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -4.637133679352701e-05}, {"token": " sound", "logprob": -0.259543716907501